From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 03:10:57 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] virtual: x18-patch the code a native child decrypts in place
 at once

World of Warcraft's ARM64 client decrypts all of its .text in place at
once, with no flush on the way, and then calls FlushInstructionCache on
each of its image's three views. Of a flushed range the x18 patcher
took as code only what its first word reaches (0264), which for the
whole image is nothing: the decrypted code kept its `cmp x18, #imm`
guards, and with x18 zeroed by iOS the client spun in one of them
(+0x33a450, a `b.lo` back to its loop) inside a TLS callback.

A cipher 2 run now also gets a code hint: the function starts the
image's .pdata lists, and the 1 KB windows of .text in which a quarter
of the words differ from the backing store of the remap the copy took
them from. What those function starts reach, following BL calls too,
is code, without leaving the rewritten windows: a window still
encrypted, or patched at load and unchanged, is left as it is. The walk
queues a word once, on a stack that grows as needed instead of one word
of stack a word of text.

Linear: PLA-514
Class: ios-port
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-31-wow-arm64-start.md, tools/tests/test_x18_encrypted.py
Offered-upstream: no
---
